## Bug Report: Notification creation allows caller to override id and read fields

### Description
In `apps/api/src/services/notificationService.js`, the `createNotification` function spreads the caller-supplied `payload` **after** setting `id` and `read`, which means a malicious or buggy caller can override essential internal fields.

### Steps to Reproduce
1. Call the notification creation endpoint with a body that includes `{ "id": "custom_id", "read": true, ... }`
2. The notification is stored with the caller-supplied `id` and `read=true` instead of the generated values

### Expected Behavior
The generated `id` and default `read: false` should always win regardless of what the caller sends.

### Affected File
`apps/api/src/services/notificationService.js`

### Current Code
```js
export async function createNotification(payload) {
  const notification = { id: `ntf_${Date.now()}`, read: false, ...payload };
  notifications.push(notification);
  return notification;
}
```

### Proposed Fix
Strip `id` and `read` from the payload before spreading:
```js
export async function createNotification(payload) {
  const { id: _id, read: _read, ...safePayload } = payload;
  const notification = { ...safePayload, id: `ntf_${Date.now()}`, read: false };
  notifications.push(notification);
  return notification;
}
```

This issue is limited only to the creator of this issue. This means that only the issue author can attempt to solve this issue. If you would like to work on it, please create another issue with the same contents and refer to issue #743 for more information.

